 acceleration is given by the slope of the line. So, we need to find the times for which the slope is negative.\n\nWe see from the graph that the slope is negative from $t=0\\,\\text{min}$ to $t=3\\,\\text{min}.$ Therefore, the light aircraft is decelerating in this time interval." 13194139533888 true] [17592186263552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263553 13194139533888 true] [17592186263552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263554 13194139533888 true] [17592186263552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263555 13194139533888 true] [17592186263552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263556 13194139533888 true] [17592186263552 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263557 13194139533888 true] [17592186263552 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533888 true] [17592186263552 1041 "selection" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263552 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263555 13194139533888 true] [17592186263552 1110 #uuid "d22b5d9d-8c03-5c12-916c-489df846e5ec" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263553 1034 "From $0\\,\\text{min}$ to $4\\,\\text{min}$" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263553 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533888 true] [17592186263553 1111 #uuid "59996675-dce6-570b-8fdf-05a24af305c5" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263554 1034 "From $3\\,\\text{min}$ to $4\\,\\text{min}$" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263554 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533888 true] [17592186263554 1111 #uuid "11a348bb-d775-557d-98a5-1edb4dd3b2c0" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263555 1034 "From $0\\,\\text{min}$ to $3\\,\\text{min}$" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263555 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533888 true] [17592186263555 1111 #uuid "39de6c91-fb21-5bae-9d55-4e8fc1cb3790" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263556 1034 "From $3\\,\\text{min}$ to $6\\,\\text{min}$" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263556 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533888 true] [17592186263556 1111 #uuid "3c54109a-bacc-512d-a290-227e99a18375" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263557 1034 "From $4\\,\\text{min}$ to $6\\,\\text{min}$" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263557 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533888 true] [17592186263557 1111 #uuid "1f464751-5d38-5f48-9903-e2b1bda0b7a4" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263558 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263559 13194139533888 true] [17592186263558 1089 "q-108468" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263558 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533888 true] [17592186263558 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/94807e919e7e2cb3f281e53de88334bd6d50dadb1de93beb2a8adc04148c5756.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph shown above represents the motion of an ostrich. At what times is the ostrich decelerating?" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263558 1113 #uuid "4fae0faa-74b4-5ebd-a36c-c55aba178d73" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263558 1145 "The ostrich is decelerating when its acceleration is negative. In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line. So, we need to find the times for which the slope is negative.\n\nWe see from the graph that the slope is negative from $t=5\\,\\text{s}$ to $t=8\\,\\text{s}.$ Therefore, the ostrich is decelerating in this time interval." 13194139533888 true] [17592186263559 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263560 13194139533888 true] [17592186263559 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263561 13194139533888 true] [17592186263559 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263562 13194139533888 true] [17592186263559 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263563 13194139533888 true] [17592186263559 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263564 13194139533888 true] [17592186263559 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533888 true] [17592186263559 1041 "selection" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263559 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263564 13194139533888 true] [17592186263559 1110 #uuid "fd2099af-465d-544f-960f-1a7f84ca3251" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263560 1034 "From $0\\,\\text{s}$ to $5\\,\\text{s}$" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263560 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533888 true] [17592186263560 1111 #uuid "6bf54c34-c89c-53a1-8b3b-183b950a56c0" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263561 1034 "From $8\\,\\text{s}$ to $10\\,\\text{s}$" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263561 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533888 true] [17592186263561 1111 #uuid "e54a8f54-54c6-52ab-8285-d352fc60f1dd" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263562 1034 "From $0\\,\\text{s}$ to $8\\,\\text{s}$" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263562 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533888 true] [17592186263562 1111 #uuid "d6aba4cc-0d51-5483-855b-17ff4186ce4f" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263563 1034 "From $5\\,\\text{s}$ to $10\\,\\text{s}$" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263563 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533888 true] [17592186263563 1111 #uuid "b73b7795-3c87-5bcb-902f-d651b77d5426" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263564 1034 "From $5\\,\\text{s}$ to $8\\,\\text{s}$" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263564 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533888 true] [17592186263564 1111 #uuid "78aa1668-d199-5937-b6e1-a262d6c5ea2d" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263565 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263566 13194139533888 true] [17592186263565 1089 "q-108567" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263565 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533888 true] [17592186263565 1100 "A particle accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $1\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$ for the first $3$ hours of its motion. It then travels at a constant speed for the next $3$ hours before accelerating with a constant acceleration of $0.5\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$ for other $3$ hours. Which graph represents the described motion?" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263565 1113 #uuid "0e2fe401-8d6a-539f-989f-5022eb0fe1eb" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263565 1145 "We start the first line from the point $(0,0),$ since the particle starts from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $1\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2},$ we draw a line with slope $1.$ \n\nHowever, because the particle is only accelerating for the first $3$ hours, we stop the line at $t=3.$ At this time, the particle's speed has increased by $1\\cdot 3=3\\,\\text{km/h}.$ So, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(3,3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/7a9fd32e5705860064e597fcf9308fee457063e060d3adede260f109ca488201.png)\n\nThe particle then travels at a constant speed for the next $3$ hours. So, the speed remains at $3\\,\\text{km/h}$ for the next $3$ hours, until $t=3+3=6.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/e4c7e47150bf1c205c243c6fa1d0f0e24b7a69f27a8461791d495eb7487cf4f3.png)\n\nAfter $t=3,$ the particle has an acceleration of $0.5\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2},$ so the slope of the third line segment is $0.5.$ The third line segment stops after $3$ hours. To figure out the velocity at this moment, we need to find the equation for the third line segment.\n\nSince the third line segment passes through the point $(6,3)$ and has a slope of $0.5,$ we can use point-slope form to write the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{v}_{1} & =m(t-{t}_{1}) \\\\ v-3 & =0.5(t-6) \\\\ v-3 & =0.5t-3 \\\\ v & =0.5t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third line segment stops when $t=6+3=9$ hours, and the velocity at this moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =0.5t \\\\ v & =0.5(9) \\\\ v & =4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third line segment stops at the point $(9,4.5).$ The final graph is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/a3fb0b9ebb6ef907a77c3d8ce139802dbd72fab04ea0bfc5db57e9f028c263b9.png)" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263567 13194139533888 true] [17592186263566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263568 13194139533888 true] [17592186263566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263569 13194139533888 true] [17592186263566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263570 13194139533888 true] [17592186263566 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263571 13194139533888 true] [17592186263566 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533888 true] [17592186263566 1041 "selection" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263566 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263567 13194139533888 true] [17592186263566 1110 #uuid "8c930349-7cee-506d-9bd7-6c4a9268c50c" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263567 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/a3fb0b9ebb6ef907a77c3d8ce139802dbd72fab04ea0bfc5db57e9f028c263b9.png" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263567 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533888 true] [17592186263567 1111 #uuid "3706b256-e76c-5c35-a28e-24d9c25191e4" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263568 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/1280bba15fdb6ada20f6d6f2ea4cfeeb39e9366939d72a06059062cc2abedc8d.png" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263568 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533888 true] [17592186263568 1111 #uuid "c807cfdc-3ed2-5b23-afa3-7b95c67ff848" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263569 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/8dd2eefb2c2397f10d4c07853ef819b8682e448824bf6e26302d4a16bc02183c.png" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263569 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533888 true] [17592186263569 1111 #uuid "9dd77db9-cd95-5827-abb5-a7923137f13c" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263570 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/170dea566e8c74e1c3df3c087c6565c85dd03c0703c4dbec1be594ca653c6aa1.png" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263570 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533888 true] [17592186263570 1111 #uuid "18e28070-d7b3-5b29-917b-d46773ca9cef" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263571 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/0c89f7f469830ff7c505d22677f6ea612f1fff396c73aba325b4e87f6f61ae42.png" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263571 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533888 true] [17592186263571 1111 #uuid "a0753367-5cbc-5c45-a98d-2965def5e03f" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263572 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263573 13194139533888 true] [17592186263572 1089 "q-59502" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263572 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533888 true] [17592186263572 1100 "A cyclist accelerates with constant acceleration from $0\\,\\text{m/s}$ to $20\\,\\text{m/s}$ in $10$ seconds. Find their acceleration." 13194139533888 true] [17592186263572 1113 #uuid "788edb6f-366d-5629-97bf-4997d96b4e3c" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263572 1145 "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph.  To plot a speed-time graph, we need two points.\n\n- The cyclist starts at a speed of $0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ This corresponds to the point $(0,0).$\n\n- In $10$ seconds, the cyclist reaches a speed of $20\\,\\text{m/s}.$ This corresponds to the point $(10,20).$\n\nSince the acceleration is constant, we plot the two points and draw a line through them as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/e60d9d6898bd8d49fb32860c439cb022c25850ff609b80ee486e71b48203b5fc.png)\n\nThe slope of the cyclist's speed-time graph gives its acceleration. The slope of this line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{20\\text{ m/s}-0\\text{ m/s}}{10\\text{ s}-0\\text{ s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\text{ m/s}}{10\\text{ s}} \\\\  & =2\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cyclist's acceleration is $2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263573 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263574 13194139533888 true] [17592186263573 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263575 13194139533888 true] [17592186263573 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263576 13194139533888 true] [17592186263573 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263577 13194139533888 true] [17592186263573 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263578 13194139533888 true] [17592186263573 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533888 true] [17592186263573 1041 "selection" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263573 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263576 13194139533888 true] [17592186263573 1110 #uuid "98838d46-b56b-5a11-bde7-4d3c9b5380ed" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263574 1034 "1{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263574 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263574 1111 #uuid "c2df05a7-1378-5172-936a-0e4daa2cbd9d" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263575 1034 "4{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263575 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263575 1111 #uuid "7473e063-1dec-5368-96c1-cc1a3481e6d0" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263576 1034 "2{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263576 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263576 1111 #uuid "f838fabb-5303-55cc-8d4b-78b6576a9962" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263577 1034 "5{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263577 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263577 1111 #uuid "064eb552-5de7-52ea-9f1e-c7ae684ff7cc" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263578 1034 "10{\\text{ m/s}}^{2}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263578 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263578 1111 #uuid "0f66a2ce-166f-5c95-b30a-ae4774bf46b9" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263579 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263580 13194139533888 true] [17592186263579 1089 "q-59709" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263579 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533888 true] [17592186263579 1100 "If the speed of a surfer is measured in $\\text{m/s},$ what are the units of its acceleration?" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263579 1113 #uuid "af168475-a930-579e-8b8b-a9480bab7d49" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263579 1145 "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph. So, let's draw an example of a speed-time graph where the speed is measured in $\\text{m/s}$ and the time is measured in $\\text{s}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/4d419f405914437764e854b63682ddb6fa4c371730a2ec1cf4263b0afd2a847e.png)\n\nIn this case, the slope is computed as\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{\\text{change in speed}}{\\text{change in time}},\n$$\n\nand so the units of the slope are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{units of speed}}{\\text{units of time}}=\\frac{\\text{m/s}}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the units of acceleration are $\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263581 13194139533888 true] [17592186263580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263582 13194139533888 true] [17592186263580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263583 13194139533888 true] [17592186263580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263584 13194139533888 true] [17592186263580 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263585 13194139533888 true] [17592186263580 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533888 true] [17592186263580 1041 "selection" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263580 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263584 13194139533888 true] [17592186263580 1110 #uuid "84057b02-37fd-5761-b8ad-cfa5406a690d" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263581 1034 "\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263581 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263581 1111 #uuid "d7f1c5ef-c6e0-5b24-b367-7eb985c5d226" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263582 1034 "\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{3}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263582 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263582 1111 #uuid "b4bb014e-a866-5a3b-8ca3-f6c9c851e3b7" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263583 1034 "\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263583 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263583 1111 #uuid "5656e317-f2f5-5327-8d15-9c41f4deb49b" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263584 1034 "\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263584 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263584 1111 #uuid "ad041e87-a577-5cfb-9e54-840eae77efcb" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263585 1034 "\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263585 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263585 1111 #uuid "a702f35e-a01a-501c-8997-7659cd5aba76" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263586 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263587 13194139533888 true] [17592186263586 1089 "q-59711" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263586 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533888 true] [17592186263586 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/40143c20622029d70a978310a7d38bc41d7b8411151d3d5f61a3b280e5807841.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph shown above represents the motion of an object. At what times is the object accelerating?" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263586 1113 #uuid "cca94ba1-21b2-5b66-81e8-07381d34c86b" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263586 1145 "The object is accelerating when its acceleration is positive. In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line. So, we need to find the times for which the slope is positive.\n\nWe see from the graph that the slope is positive from $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ to $t=30\\,\\text{s}.$ Therefore, the object is accelerating in this time interval." 13194139533888 true] [17592186263587 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263588 13194139533888 true] [17592186263587 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263589 13194139533888 true] [17592186263587 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263590 13194139533888 true] [17592186263587 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263591 13194139533888 true] [17592186263587 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263592 13194139533888 true] [17592186263587 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533888 true] [17592186263587 1041 "selection" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263587 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263592 13194139533888 true] [17592186263587 1110 #uuid "377113c7-0809-5b74-92bc-970b36c9eb83" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263588 1034 "From $70\\,\\text{s}$ to $80\\,\\text{s}$" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263588 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533888 true] [17592186263588 1111 #uuid "c70a9519-a240-5075-9773-3d95df09ba0a" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263589 1034 "From $30\\,\\text{s}$ to $80\\,\\text{s}$" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263589 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533888 true] [17592186263589 1111 #uuid "8a52a03e-e58b-5b97-b8b6-6922fa92d2c9" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263590 1034 "From $30\\,\\text{s}$ to $70\\,\\text{s}$" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263590 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533888 true] [17592186263590 1111 #uuid "906aab22-f035-538a-b5ff-faf6115b51fa" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263591 1034 "From $0\\,\\text{s}$ to $70\\,\\text{s}$" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263591 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533888 true] [17592186263591 1111 #uuid "ef489df5-9d55-5642-a7f9-138ff2bd96d2" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263592 1034 "From $0\\,\\text{s}$ to $30\\,\\text{s}$" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263592 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533888 true] [17592186263592 1111 #uuid "b2ffe1d3-964e-5537-9b57-183d923dd996" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263593 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263594 13194139533888 true] [17592186263593 1089 "q-59714" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263593 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533888 true] [17592186263593 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/0bfadc49b3d5d846641b78e07a6ec279b11da13e6f69dec3f1f054e03d5d3f6d.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph shown above represents the motion of a windsurfer. At what times is the windsurfer accelerating?" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263593 1113 #uuid "e0f780b9-e217-582a-b4c6-39ce247aadff" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263593 1145 "The windsurfer is accelerating when their acceleration is positive. In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line. So, we need to find the times for which the slope is positive.\n\nWe see from the graph that the slope is positive from $t=0\\,\\text{s}$ to $t=3\\,\\text{s}$ and from $t=5\\,\\text{s}$ to $t=7\\,\\text{s}.$ Therefore, the motorcycle is windsurfer in this time interval." 13194139533888 true] [17592186263594 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263595 13194139533888 