edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533888 true] [17592186263600 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/700081e764cbf4be23f702b1c136ba2e4b6b1f460c96f57e2898c3ac070e62bb.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph represents the motion of a surfer. Calculate the acceleration of the surfer." 13194139533888 true] [17592186263600 1113 #uuid "e3137b36-6d19-5c82-80f3-7ce7d93599be" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263600 1145 "In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line.\n\nThe line passes through the points $(0,0)$ and $(8,5),$ so its slope is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{5\\text{ m/s}-0\\text{ m/s}}{8\\text{ s}-0\\text{ s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\text{ m/s}}{8\\text{ s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{8}\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration of the surfer is $\\frac{5}{8}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263601 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263602 13194139533888 true] [17592186263601 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263603 13194139533888 true] [17592186263601 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263604 13194139533888 true] [17592186263601 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263605 13194139533888 true] [17592186263601 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263606 13194139533888 true] [17592186263601 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533888 true] [17592186263601 1041 "selection" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263601 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263602 13194139533888 true] [17592186263601 1110 #uuid "6d6356c4-e0e8-5ece-8e38-7d0011279fde" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263602 1034 "\\frac{5}{8}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263602 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263602 1111 #uuid "904c8abf-e5af-59d0-86f5-c5752de23468" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263603 1034 "\\frac{3}{8}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263603 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263603 1111 #uuid "f17bc2dc-c8ff-5573-bb4c-2398c1104a2a" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263604 1034 "\\frac{3}{5}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263604 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263604 1111 #uuid "d383b76d-7fe0-5500-baaa-ff7247df1f0c" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263605 1034 "\\frac{8}{5}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263605 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263605 1111 #uuid "8a3dfe79-0aa2-5776-b0de-d5cfc3b10d10" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263606 1034 "\\frac{1}{8}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263606 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263606 1111 #uuid "6b5d046d-da80-5f47-a99c-07ef0cfb912a" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263607 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263608 13194139533888 true] [17592186263607 1089 "q-59749" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263607 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533888 true] [17592186263607 1100 "A cyclist accelerates with a constant acceleration from $2\\,\\text{m/s}$ to $12\\,\\text{m/s}$ in $4$ seconds. Find their acceleration." 13194139533888 true] [17592186263607 1113 #uuid "f3681f60-e73d-553c-92d2-8946be3cb315" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263607 1145 "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph.  To plot a speed-time graph, we need two points.\n\n- The cyclist starts at a speed of $2\\,\\text{m/s}.$ This corresponds to the point $(0,2).$\n\n- In $4$ seconds, the cyclist reaches a speed of $12\\,\\text{m/s}.$ This corresponds to the point $(4,12).$\n\nSince the acceleration is constant, we plot the two points and draw a line through them as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/d1a813ebcbfa04b42729d60b73bc439a2060aafae8430c60984ba1772e940364.png)\n\nThe slope of the cyclist's speed-time graph gives their acceleration. The slope of this line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{12\\text{ m/s}-2\\text{ m/s}}{4\\text{s}-0\\text{s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\text{ m/s}}{4\\text{ s}} \\\\  & =2.5\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cyclist's acceleration is $2.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263608 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263609 13194139533888 true] [17592186263608 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263610 13194139533888 true] [17592186263608 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263611 13194139533888 true] [17592186263608 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263612 13194139533888 true] [17592186263608 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263613 13194139533888 true] [17592186263608 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533888 true] [17592186263608 1041 "selection" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263608 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263612 13194139533888 true] [17592186263608 1110 #uuid "7b726e35-2998-5856-be8f-0b76d1180dc8" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263609 1034 "3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263609 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263609 1111 #uuid "381bb04e-5bdb-5dfe-bc1f-de78472f064d" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263610 1034 "3.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263610 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263610 1111 #uuid "ca4dc282-4fbe-5f15-acc5-deffa034334c" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263611 1034 "2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263611 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263611 1111 #uuid "0ad0c358-0022-522d-a373-178f2392f3fd" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263612 1034 "2.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263612 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263612 1111 #uuid "b977adf4-f4ba-5c82-9a98-669c1dbb2000" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263613 1034 "4\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263613 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263613 1111 #uuid "bca52c30-480b-5645-9def-8226a27baafd" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263614 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263615 13194139533888 true] [17592186263614 1089 "q-59760" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263614 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533888 true] [17592186263614 1100 "A particle accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $5\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$ for the first $4$ hours of its motion. It then travels at a constant speed for the next $6$ hours. Which graph represents the described motion?" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263614 1113 #uuid "3482860b-89cd-5fe9-aa14-3b6ba9d6957f" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263614 1145 "We start the first line from the point $(0,0),$ since the particle starts from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $5\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2},$ we draw a line with slope $5.$ \n\nHowever, because the particle is only accelerating for the first $4$ hours, we stop the line at $t=4.$ At this time, the particle's speed has increased by $4\\cdot 5=20\\text{ km/h}.$ So, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(4,20).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/cf0471f38486963f4237b4fab7c1cd109d02cc2d7ac3e30990831654f04c5b11.png)\n\nThe particle then travels at a constant speed for the next $6$ hours. So, the speed remains at $20\\,\\text{km/h}$ for the next $6$ hours, until $t=4+6=10.$\n\nSo, the second line segment stops at the point $(10,20).$ The final graph is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/fae4c7d8cfc79dd70041ebd697226408a5cdd0676ad2ddfaad1500ea1ba0a76e.png)" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263615 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263616 13194139533888 true] [17592186263615 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263617 13194139533888 true] [17592186263615 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263618 13194139533888 true] [17592186263615 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263619 13194139533888 true] [17592186263615 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263620 13194139533888 true] [17592186263615 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533888 true] [17592186263615 1041 "selection" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263615 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263616 13194139533888 true] [17592186263615 1110 #uuid "be97c182-d747-5fd5-a932-3391e9e53795" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263616 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/fae4c7d8cfc79dd70041ebd697226408a5cdd0676ad2ddfaad1500ea1ba0a76e.png" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263616 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533888 true] [17592186263616 1111 #uuid "191eeee1-b661-51a1-b6e4-9127696b474c" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263617 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/d1123b8a9a86288380e084f8001ef5ebcc160d5647c83f3878a3d7b357087a9a.png" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263617 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533888 true] [17592186263617 1111 #uuid "43c1a9a4-1553-5b82-a4e1-d59493d4622f" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263618 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/7c3e6680c0a158181e11b473a94fa43b1a2afb3ea5c6206244801f4830bafff8.png" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263618 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533888 true] [17592186263618 1111 #uuid "ebd047f9-c7f2-5820-8567-c1741e0ae2a4" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263619 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/11691073c02c80a9c4400623a8ee648d8a9405faab89ac8ce4fdbb7dbdd61baa.png" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263619 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533888 true] [17592186263619 1111 #uuid "19cb75f0-a33b-5df0-9c1e-6052290b754b" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263620 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/ec8ff99395b5f33f531303acd91c58ada3b7f71213a5af5c76c86dae62d277e9.png" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263620 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533888 true] [17592186263620 1111 #uuid "b650d23d-75fa-5b40-9dce-dcf65c8a210c" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263621 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263622 13194139533888 true] [17592186263621 1089 "q-91439" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263621 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533888 true] [17592186263621 1100 "If the speed of an object is measured in $\\text{cm/s},$ what are the units of its acceleration?" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263621 1113 #uuid "32993449-a772-5d35-ad65-f08d575152d5" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263621 1145 "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph. So, let's draw an example of a speed-time graph where the speed is measured in $\\text{cm/s}$ and the time is measured in $\\text{s}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/f97581877814a2626d820b07b4e7a2c3d637f9369b119d0e0c3896c77e15f964.png)\n\nIn this case, the slope is computed as\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{\\text{change in speed}}{\\text{change in time}},\n$$\n\nand so the units of the slope are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{units of speed}}{\\text{units of time}}=\\frac{\\text{cm/s}}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the units of acceleration are $\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263622 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263623 13194139533888 true] [17592186263622 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263624 13194139533888 true] [17592186263622 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263625 13194139533888 true] [17592186263622 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263626 13194139533888 true] [17592186263622 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263627 13194139533888 true] [17592186263622 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533888 true] [17592186263622 1041 "selection" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263622 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263626 13194139533888 true] [17592186263622 1110 #uuid "a27454ab-8e79-5daa-9d39-d80fb1fda56c" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263623 1034 "\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263623 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263623 1111 #uuid "85986805-9eb9-571d-8eab-436cb4c6bc35" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263624 1034 "\\frac{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263624 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263624 1111 #uuid "5587e043-9304-5405-93be-c58b2410f20a" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263625 1034 "\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{3}}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263625 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263625 1111 #uuid "f2291df0-8ab3-52ce-8fda-1513ad595073" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263626 1034 "\\frac{\\text{cm}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263626 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263626 1111 #uuid "f692c834-e620-5135-9193-26d7fcee5b96" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263627 1034 "\\frac{{\\text{cm}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263627 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533888 true] [17592186263627 1111 #uuid "cfc97691-63d5-5aab-99d7-571e3927d025" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263628 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263629 13194139533888 true] [17592186263628 1089 "q-91537" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263628 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533888 true] [17592186263628 1100 "A man accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $0.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ for the first $10$ seconds of his motion. He then jogs at a constant speed for the next $20$ seconds before accelerating with a constant acceleration of $0.1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ for another $20$ seconds. Which graph represents the described motion?" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263628 1113 #uuid "b0137214-9cb6-52dd-8cad-70440ba49638" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263628 1145 "We start the first line from the point $(0,0),$ since the man starts from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $0.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ we draw a line with slope $0.3.$ \n\nHowever, because the man is only accelerating for the first $10$ seconds, we stop the line at $t=10.$ At this time, the man's speed has increased by $0.3\\cdot 10=3\\text{ m/s}.$ So, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(10,3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/4a6d3b2d3f1664aed35ea11b064f6f75a3707a2051414ca9015a690148f06c16.png)\n\nThe man then travels at a constant speed for the next $20$ seconds. So, the speed remains at $3\\,\\text{m/s}$ for the next $20$ seconds, until $t=10+20=30.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/1b579d714470aa484c23447647864b165c5b453e56e56262ace4c6d5d6b7d5f3.png)\n\nAfter $t=30$, the man has an acceleration of $0.1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ so the slope of the third line segment is $0.1.$ The third line segment stops after $20$ seconds. To figure out the velocity at this moment, we need to find the equation for the third line segment.\n\nSince the third line segment passes through the point $(30,3)$ and has a slope of $0.1,$ we can use point-slope form to write the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{v}_{1} & =m(t-{t}_{1}) \\\\ v-3 & =0.1(t-30) \\\\ v-3 & =0.1t-3 \\\\ v & =0.1t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third line segment stops when $t=30+20=50$ seconds, and the velocity at this moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =0.1t \\\\ v & =0.1(50) \\\\ v & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third line segment stops at the point $(50,5).$ The final graph is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/bd9c1df348dffaefb5882d6414f3e73e0ebf93c3c2ffa6275cab2ba89b11f88f.png)" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263629 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263630 13194139533888 true] [17592186263629 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263631 13194139533888 true] [17592186263629 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263632 13194139533888 true] [17592186263629 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263633 13194139533888 true] [17592186263629 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263634 13194139533888 true] [17592186263629 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533888 true] [17592186263629 1041 "selection" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263629 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263630 13194139533888 true] [17592186263629 1110 #uuid "f4eb0a6b-0aa2-5e9b-8bc7-86fba7d5e43d" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263630 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/bd9c1df348dffaefb5882d6414f3e73e0ebf93c3c2ffa6275cab2ba89b11f88f.png" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263630 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533888 true] [17592186263630 1111 #uuid "f3fa526c-68a7-5a81-a6f8-edb96f0a9212" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263631 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/31477705bc3c0daa54aa8dda45680d1d8381d2bd720d920868780796f58f05bf.png" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263631 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533888 true] [17592186263631 1111 #uuid "0ff10561-fbf1-52a4-8777-c0e00d9bccaf" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263632 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/bf57601cb3fd2fc47b4b69947acc8311f25100655b9f8e40b906b8c2af8502cf.png" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263632 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533888 true] [17592186263632 1111 #uuid "f772a2bd-3055-569f-ba38-8193320172f0" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263633 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/32aa87f487c2d0082e0e3ceeebf037d8cdb3052a726fc5b43902d621dfdec790.png" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263633 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533888 true] [17592186263633 1111 #uuid "c98edf1e-ef62-5c5c-9266-82a9e374a5a1" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263634 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/3d4a5d8faed32c63b39d7ca288030b27ce35bb4bfe1fbccb47072ec45ea2c2d3.png" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263634 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533888 true] [17592186263634 1111 #uuid "cb6a5bff-0668-54ac-ac2a-8f9a08e49f52" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263635 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263636 13194139533888 true] [17592186263635 1089 "q-91547" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263635 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533888 true] [17592186263635 1100 "A school bus accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $30\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$ for the first $2$ hours of its motion. It then travels at a constant speed for the next $2$ hours before decelerating with a constant deceleration of $20\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$ until it stops. Which graph represents the described motion?" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263635 1113 #uuid "ee3c269b-662d-5df4-aa3f-23055e6c89fe" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263635 1145 "We start the first line from the point $(0,0),$ since the school bus starts from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $30\\,{\\text{km/s}}^{2},$ we draw a line with slope $30.$ \n\nHowever, because the school bus is only accelerating for the first $2$ hours, we stop the line at $t=2.$ At this time, the school bus's speed has increased by $2\\cdot 30=60\\text{ km/h}.$ So, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(2,60).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/2126d713b00d66abb0d06e5878fdadc543c9c8a3a0000eb2f56b1048a2824ca4.png)\n\nThe school bus then travels at a constant speed for the next $2$ hours. So, the speed remains at $60\\,\\text{km/h}$ for the next $2$ hours, until $t=2+2=4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/6512a9d80ca9de16f5384031590b7ae5e58a9a2871bd5ac7aace1803f0d82603.png)\n\nAfter $t=4$, the school bus has a deceleration of $20\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2},$ so the slope of the third line segment is $-20.$ The third line segment stops when the school bus's speed returns to $0.$ To figure out when this is, we need to find the equation for the third line segment.\n\nSince the third line segment passes through the point $(4,60)$ and has a slope of $-20,$ we can use point-slope form to write the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{v}_{1} & =m(t-{t}_{1}) \\\\ v-60 & =-20(t-4) \\\\ v-60 & =-20t+80 \\\\ v & =-20t+140\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve for $t$ when the speed of the school bus is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-20t+140 \\\\ 0 & =-20t+140 \\\\ 20t & =140 \\\\ t & =\\frac{140}{20} \\\\ t & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third line segment stops at the point $(7,0).$ The final graph is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/2305c2241cbd9b7f8da60f9aa377f9068a4bb42cf57f38a2ad6203ba7f325d42.png)" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263636 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263637 13194139533888 true] [17592186263636 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263638 13194139533888 true] [17592186263636 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263639 13194139533888 true] [17592186263636 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263640 13194139533888 true] [17592186263636 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263641 13194139533888 true] [17592186263636 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533888 true] [17592186263636 1041 "selection" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263636 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263639 13194139533888 true] [17592186263636 1110 #uuid "145c2b2b-a152-5682-a275-3cc9fe597105" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263637 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/60491436cdf458f0f71ccaa114120150f4a7bd54552e99d95a169c5149765b98.png" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263637 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533888 true] [17592186263637 1111 #uuid "478f4adc-b642-5f2a-ae49-cbe9ab45ee81" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263638 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/02f41321ebec8a6a564fbd17ee9f86385817b7f767fd56fe9020a45ca2c39338.png" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263638 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533888 true] [17592186263638 1111 #uuid "abb091c4-dd31-5753-b9bd-0c469190b11a" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263639 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/2305c2241cbd9b7f8da60f9aa377f9068a4bb42cf57f38a2ad6203ba7f325d42.png" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263639 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533888 true] [17592186263639 1111 #uuid "a751bd80-f6b8-5982-871d-4cd2115b136e" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263640 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/e20692bb628a6ceebc9b3ce1ae89508c79b4f07da9a97e92a847ca9d9e958b45.png" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263640 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533888 true] [17592186263640 1111 #uuid "e9e6daa0-bcf8-5286-95ad-3eef5e99eb7a" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263641 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/d9b3b459f9dbe86701640a1ee01f100efe057e0aa2d656679e6761e9338d9edb.png" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263641 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139533888 true] [17592186263641 1111 #uuid "105ccda9-840a-51bf-ba6f-ea4ae61501b1" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263642 1017 #edb/ref 17592186263643 13194139533888 true] [17592186263642 1089 "q-91549" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263642 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533888 true] [17592186263642 1100 "A train accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ for the first $10$ seconds of its motion. Then the train decelerates with a constant deceleration of $1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ for the next $15$ seconds, before traveling at a constant speed for the next $20$ seconds. Which graph represents the described motion?" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263642 1113 #uuid "7fbae218-1c51-5854-8184-ffd9f720ee73" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263642 1145 "We start the first line from the point $(0,0),$ since the train starts from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ we draw a line with slope $3.$ \n\nHowever, because the train is only accelerating for the first $10$ seconds, we stop the line at $t=10.$ At this time, the train's speed has increased by $10\\cdot 3=30\\text{ m/s}.$ So, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(10,30).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/54ac34c5fe7833d42fb8ddda729d3823ff0e726e1e6228492639a9e91bad2762.png)\n\nAfter $t=10$, the train has a deceleration of $1\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ so the slope of the second line segment is $-1.$ The second line segment stops after $15$ seconds. To figure out the velocity at this moment, we need to find the equation for the second line segment.\n\nSince the second line segment passes through the point $(10,30)$ and has a slope of $-1,$ we can use point-slope form to write the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v-{v}_{1} & =m(t-{t}_{1}) \\\\ v-30 & =-(t-10) \\\\ v-30 & =-t+10 \\\\ v & =-t+40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second line segment stops when $t=10+15=25$ seconds, and the velocity at this moment is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-t+40 \\\\ v & =-25+40 \\\\ v & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/b199170cfdadd688a15668afdbac796a90fc158c94bfc0ffc8b9245c5a741e8e.png)\n\nThe train then travels at a constant speed for the next $20$ seconds. So, the speed remains at $15\\,\\text{m/s}$ for the next $20$ seconds, until $t=25+20=45.$\n\nFinally, the third line segment stops at the point $(45,15).$ The final graph is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/b1ece8dd03146aaf420746b6ba3cc59b3d707150fe3682bbdcd6f05d645fb4c4.png)" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263643 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263644 13194139533888 true] [17592186263643 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263645 13194139533888 true] [17592186263643 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263646 13194139533888 true] [17592186263643 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263647 13194139533888 true] [17592186263643 1000 #edb/ref 17592186263648 13194139533888 true] [17592186263643 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533888 true] [17592186263643 1041 "selection" 13194139533888 true] [17592186263643 1104 #edb/ref 17592186263645 13194139533888 true] 